 the same time  they're what movements always have to be  there in communities they're operating  globally online offline in the streets  for much more progressive transformative  politics than the democratic party maybe  even Bernie Sanders is prepared to do at  this point so my argument is that you  you really strongly need to see a kind  of universalizing dance between the  movements on the streets and the  political parties I want to give you one  one clear example of this in the United  Front idea in Germany when when fascism  came into power you had a lot of Weimar  Germany which was German at the German  Republic in the 20s it had very strong  gay feminist experimental cultural  movement that we would call identity  politics today and it had some fairly  strong liberal and left politics the  Social Democratic Party that was running  Germany and by Maher for a while and  kind of insane Communist Party that was  very under the influence of stone  well Hitler came into power because the  movements remained separate from the  social democratic and socialist parties  and remained fragmented from each other  and so you didn't get the United Front  whereas the far right which chiller was  one of many car wreck people began to  coordinate with the German aristocracy  at the time and create a united front  with corporate and aristocratic German  elites who had run Germany before for  the by my republic and and so it's just  we're in a period that a lot of people  recognize is authoritarian potentially  moving in a kind of neo-fascists  direction and that's where these  universalizing united resistance forces  are very very important and they failed  in Germany and I'm very you know I've  written a lot about fascism and Germany  and so I'm very conscious of this and  its idea of universalizing is partly  related to that concern that unless we  have these universalizing movements that  bring together both the movements and  the political parties and bring them  movements together and bring you know  just coordinate because you know at the  heart of this is the recognition that  global capitalism is a what academics  like used to you know like to call an  intersectional system that is there is a  reflects a system of you know dominant  power hierarchies which are  interconnected race and capitalism are  not the same thing you can have racism  in other economic systems and there can  capitalism potentially without racism  both hard to imagine America but they  are really intertwined like DNA  DNA strands so in you're going to catch  the system you need to recognize and  black feminists like well whole bunch of  them but people who leading black lives  matter now and so for to call themselves  socialists there you're seeing in a lot  of these movements in the environmental  move you're seeing Bill McKibben who was  very insular and sidle and we started  with a lot of his work on three  fifty.org environment but has moved much  more to understanding the connection I  wrote a book called read to green  earlier and he blurred that book because  he was beginning to recognize that you  couldn't deal with climate change unless  you deal with capitalist and Charles I'm  curious and we have we have so much to  get to we actually are starting to sort  of run out of time even in the second  part and and I had a dozen or so  questions we were at we got a really  good answer to one but there's a couple  other things I do want to touch on  you're sort of talking about the sort of  positioning of what the left should have  in American politics and I've been  pretty critical of Democrats who have  run basically on being Republican light  in many ways can you address that a  little bit and whether it's time to  really just be definitively left  absolutely I mean this started with the  Clinton years when the Reagan people  came in they changed they tried to  undermine remember from fifty years  prior to Reagan eight nineteen eighty  you had a New Deal I called regime a new  deal of American politics which operated  under the system of really an effort to  move capitalism as far as possible  towards european-style  Scandinavian style you know justice for  workers and bringing together all kinds  of social justice issues that element  Roosevelt human rights universal idea  immigration so but Reagan knocked that  out and then the Clintons came along and  explicitly said were no longer the  Democratic Party is no longer a party of  the New Deal we're not for big  government were not for Social Welfare  and so forth and they did these very  incremental changes and moved heavily  toward an identity kind of politics  which was stripped of its class  sensibilities and awarenesses this has  gone on and and you know Obama  ironically in some way maintained this  he thought I think he felt he's a  cautious politician he  symbolised you know hope and change but  he really continued the Clinton strategy  of kind of moving away from these  economic justice system shaking kind of  politics toward um water I call it Bush  and Bush lighter but to party we don't  have a Labor Party we don't have a real  progressive party in United States like  there are in Europe so I feel yes to  your question this is a period and  that's why Sanders and you know people  like Elizabeth Warren my senator so far  are so important because they represent  a new energy within the progressive wing  of the Democratic Party a lot of them of  the black caucus the Progressive Caucus  on the party you know indivisible  there's a whole set of new grassroots  younger millennial people who are going  to push the Democratic Party to the left  and this is the moment and you mentioned  David the public opinion supports that  you among Millennials more Millennials  say they are positive toward the idea of  democratic socialism I think they're  thinking about Sanders sort of style  rather and they're negative about  capitalism so if you look at public  opinion polls which I've put into the  book just to punctuate this point there  is enormous public support for these  progressive positions the Democrats can  win by highlighting these issues whether  whether it were they having to do with  you know medical care for all or social  security things which the Republicans  are going to be going after or whether  it's unions minimum wage the role of  money and politics that you know  citizens united the public opinion is  there for the Democratic Party to really  move to the left but the Democratic  Party since Clinton has been funded by  Wall Street and by and the big  Democratic funders who will play an  enormous role in where mainstream  parties go have been retarding that and  they found candidates in the Clintons  and even in Obama who were sort of  reconciled themselves to these  traditional limits of the Democrats  since Clinton and this is the moment for  change for transformative change and you  know we're in a period of emergency  climate change weather nuclear  impossibilities and Iran or Korea  whatever  don't have time to play around and to be  to be incremental about this I mean  incremental all kinds of change are good  but we need a mainstream party that is  going to shake this system up and I  think that's why Sanders who plays you  know is the most popular politician in  America today according to Gallup polls  he's got a 60% approval rating in red  states as well as blue states so the  Democrats can have a 50-state strategy  and win all over the country as long as  they bring this kind of universal  ice-cream message including to the  trumpian working class who is going to  be very disenchanted purely on economic  grounds with Trump's you know policies I  mean they might like his identity  politics that we talked about the  beginning yeah his he's really going to  bring jobs that are jobs that we've  brought to bottom all or whatever they  may bring back few jobs but they're  going to there's going to be no unions  there going to be no regulation no  minimum wage and they're going to be  terrible jobs that most of his followers  are not going to be one of doing a lot  of what we've been talking about from  the book welcome to the revolution  universalizing resistance for social  justice and democracy in perilous times  we've been speaking with the book's  author Charles durbur thanks so much for  talking to us about the book thank you  so much David I really appreciate it
 

